## Always use absolute paths — never `cd`

Your shell already starts in your workspace, so a `cd` buys you nothing
and costs you the run: the agent harness refuses to auto-approve a
compound command that pairs a `cd` with a relative file read (and, for
`grep`/`rg`/`diff`/`git`/`cp`/`mv`, refuses outright), so the session
stops on a permission dialog with no human watching it.

- Do NOT write `cd <dir> && <command>`, and do not `cd` as a separate
step either.
- Address every file and directory by its absolute path:
`cat /abs/path/to/file.rs`, `rg pattern /abs/path/to/dir`,
`sed -n '1,40p' /abs/path/to/file.rs`.
- For a tool that must run from a directory, use its own flag rather
than a `cd` — e.g. `git -C <abs dir> …`, `make -C <abs dir> …`.

This is about the command shape, not about what you may access: the
engine's guards resolve every path the same way however it is spelled,
so writing paths in full is never a way around a boundary.

## VCS

Use `jj` for all VCS. Do not invoke `git` directly except via `gh`.

- `jj git fetch` to sync; `jj new main@origin` for a fresh task;
`jj edit <bookmark>` to resume.
- `jj describe -m '...'` to set commit messages;
`jj bookmark set <name> -r @` to create or advance a bookmark.
- **NEVER push branches or open PRs with bare VCS commands** (`jj git push`,
`git push`, `gh pr create`). A PreToolUse hook blocks these. Use:
- `"$CUBE_BIN" pr create --branch <name>` — new PR (pushes branch + opens PR, jj-aware, no GIT_DIR needed)
- `"$CUBE_BIN" pr update --branch <name>` — existing PR (pushes new commits to it)
- Never `jj git push --deleted` or `git push --delete`
without explicit user approval.
- **Never run raw `git` against cube's shared source store** — the
checkout your workspace's `.jj/repo` pointer names, under cube's repos
root — whether by `cd`-ing into it or by pointing `GIT_DIR`, `--git-dir`
or `-C` at it. A PreToolUse hook refuses every git write there and every
shallow `fetch`/`pull`/`clone` (`--depth`, `--shallow-since`, …) that
would touch it: one such fetch turns the shared store into a shallow
clone and breaks every dispatch for that repository. Read-only git
against the store (`log`, `show`, `diff`, `rev-parse`, …) is allowed.
To reproduce a CI script that runs raw git, clone into a throwaway
directory under your own temp dir and run it there.
- `.claude/` is gitignored by the engine. Do not force-track
or commit anything inside it (no `--force`,
no `jj file track .claude/...`).

### Commit messages must be inline

Always pass `-m "…"` to `git commit`, `git rebase`, `jj commit`,
`jj describe`, and amend/squash flows (`git commit --amend`,
`jj squash`). The worker environment has no usable
`$EDITOR` — commands that fall through to one fail. Fix by
re-running with `-m`. For splitting on jj 0.26 through 0.46, use
`jj split --config 'ui.editor="true"' <paths>`, then
`jj describe -r <revision> -m "message"` on each half.

## Creating a PR from a jj workspace

Cube workspaces are secondary jj workspaces. There is no `.git/`
at the workspace root, so bare `gh` calls fail with
`fatal: not a git repository` (pass `-R <owner/repo>` to `gh`).
`"$CUBE_BIN" pr create` and `"$CUBE_BIN" pr update` push to GitHub by URL and
resolve `-R <owner/repo>` from `jj git remote` automatically.

### Canonical PR creation recipe

**NEVER pass a PR body or comment as `--body "<inline text>"`** — the shell
evaluates backticks, `$(...)`, and `${VAR}` inside double-quoted strings,
corrupting inline code. Always write the body to a temp file and pass `--body-file`:

```sh
jj describe -m "your commit message"
jj bookmark set my-feature -r @
body=$(mktemp)
cat > "$body" << 'PRBODY'
## Summary
Your description here. Inline code like `crate-name` and `$(cmd)` is safe.
PRBODY
"$CUBE_BIN" pr create --branch my-feature --title "Your PR title" --body-file "$body"
```

`"$CUBE_BIN" pr create` is safe to retry: if an open PR already exists for
the branch, it returns that PR's URL instead of erroring, without
pushing again. Use `"$CUBE_BIN" pr update --branch my-feature` only when
you have new commits to push onto an already-open PR (it errors if
none exists). `"$CUBE_BIN" pr create` handles the push and `--allow-new`
automatically.

### `origin` is the real GitHub upstream (shared object store)

Every cube workspace is a **secondary jj workspace** that SHARES one
object store with its siblings — there is no per-workspace clone. That
store has a single `origin` remote pointing at the real GitHub upstream.

- Because the store is shared, a `jj git fetch` in ANY workspace
advances the remote-tracking bookmarks (e.g. `main@origin`) seen by
ALL of them. Don't be alarmed if refs move without you fetching.
- A solid belt-and-suspenders check that a push actually landed is to
compare your local commit against GitHub's head sha (do not infer
success from the push command's own output alone):

```sh
# local commit you intended to ship
jj log -r my-feature --no-graph -T commit_id
# what GitHub actually has (must match)
gh api repos/<owner>/<repo>/branches/my-feature --jq .commit.sha
# for a specific PR head:
gh api repos/<owner>/<repo>/pulls/<n> --jq .head.sha
```

## Fix failing checks at the root cause

**Hard constraint — fix failing checks at the root cause; never bypass them.**
Do not suppress failures with exclusions/allowlists (`CHECKS.yaml`, checkleft), lint-disable comments, `allow_bypass` or override flags, `--no-verify` or skipped hooks, broad `#[allow(...)]` / `// swiftlint:disable` / `# noqa`, deleted/ignored/xfail/skipped tests, weakened assertions, or raised thresholds/limits. Fix the underlying code, tests, lint, or file size instead. A genuinely necessary check relaxation requires approval by the repo owner with full justification: STOP and surface it. Use the typed question flags on a blocked declaration when that channel is enabled; never decide autonomously.

## Long-running build and test commands

Bazel's own per-test timeouts (test size and timeout attributes) are what bound tests; do not wrap other build or test commands in a shell `timeout` either. A build that is still compiling with no errors is making progress, even when it is slow because other builds share the machine. If a wait bound is needed (for example a polling yield), keep polling the running command instead of killing it. If a hard cap is ever unavoidable for some other command, it must be at least two hours, and a timeout must never be treated as a reason to rerun the same command under the same cap. To diagnose a command, inspect only this invocation's output base and logs; never infer ownership or blockage from global process-name matches.

## Merge-conflict telemetry

If you ever run `"$CUBE_BIN" workspace rebase` mid-task (e.g. to sync with
`main` before pushing, or because a push was rejected) and it reports
`REBASED_WITH_CONFLICTS`, resolve the conflicts as normal, then
AFTER resolving run:

```sh
"$BOSS_BIN" engine conflicts record-producer --execution-id <your execution id> \
--head-branch <branch> --base-branch <main_branch> --files <comma-separated conflicted paths>
```

using the `branch`, `main_branch`, and conflicted-file list `cube
workspace rebase` printed (or `jj resolve --list` if you need it
again). This is telemetry only — best-effort, never blocks or
reverts your actual work — but it is the only way the engine learns
about conflicts you resolve on your own, before they'd ever reach
the reviewed in-PR conflict-resolution flow.

## Reuse before you build

Before implementing any cross-cutting capability (an API/HTTP client,
config loading, caching, logging wiring, retry/backoff, or similar
infrastructure), search the repo for an existing implementation
first. If one exists, reuse or extend it instead of writing a new
one. If duplication is genuinely necessary, say so explicitly in the
PR description with the reason — a justified, visible
exception is the only way to avoid a "revision required"
reuse/duplication finding from the automated reviewer.

## Boundaries

- Do not modify files outside this workspace. Sibling workspaces
belong to other workers.
- Do not modify cube's database, lease state, or workspace registry.
- `~/Library/Application Support/Boss/` is engine-only.
Never read, write, or touch it. Do not query the database
yourself; ask for any task or product context you need.
`bossctl` is not available to workers.

## Capturing a screenshot for your own verification

**Do not commit capture PNGs** — and do not delete them either.
Attach them:

```sh
"$BOSS_BIN" attach /tmp/shot.png --caption "wide table, after the fix"
```

The engine stores the image outside this workspace (which is
recycled after your run), for your own verification and for
someone looking at this run locally. `"$BOSS_BIN" attach --list` shows
what this task already has, across runs.

Validation is immediate and typed: a path outside this workspace or
a temp dir, a file that is not really a PNG/JPEG, an oversize render,
or a per-run cap all come back as an error you can fix and retry now,
rather than a silent failure discovered after you are gone. Nothing
is ever downscaled or truncated behind your back — an image is either
stored exactly as you rendered it or refused.

**Do not put an attachment URL — or any other localhost link — in a
PR body.** The link it prints is only reachable on this machine;
nobody reading the PR on GitHub can open it. This applies whether or
not the evidence server is listening: if it isn't, the image is
still stored, but that is still not a reason to paste a URL or inline
it as base64. Summarize registered screenshots by caption in the evidence
section; that notice does not give GitHub readers access to the images. Where
a PR body needs evidence a GitHub reader can actually open, use
something that is — a CI artifact, a deploy preview, a code
reference.

## Probes

Messages may arrive between turns. Treat them as questions from a
human reviewer — short, specific answers.

## CI failure classification

Classify a CI failure by cause, then act — do not park, and do not leave "someone else will handle it" as an available excuse:
- Caused by your change: fix it. No judgement call, not grounds to block, no handoff.
- Unrelated to your change AND trivial: fix it inline — that is what a human would do.
- Unrelated AND not trivial: do not absorb it into this PR. Do not silently expand this PR into repairing someone else's breakage — that makes the diff unreviewable and couples two unrelated changes. This is the case where stopping to escalate can be legitimate. Flag it, stating whether the remedy is a separate fix or reverting the offending commit, and why. "CI is red" is not an escalation; name the specific problem and the proposed remedy.

Whichever branch you take, the PR description must say which category you judged the failure into and on what basis. A silent inline fix of an unrelated failure is nearly as bad as ignoring it, because nobody can review a decision they cannot see.

Trivial means the correct fix is mechanical and obviously correct — the canonical case is `main` adding a required `is_review_supervisor` field to `StartWorkerInput` after a test was written, breaking clippy on an unrelated PR's merge-queue synthetic commit. One line, mechanical. A failing test whose correct behaviour you would have to reason about, or a failure whose cause you cannot identify, is not trivial, regardless of how few lines the fix looks.

The engine already mints CI-fix revisions. That path is the fallback for failures that genuinely need a separate change (the unrelated-and-not-trivial case). It is not a reason to punt a failure you could fix in this run.


## Write questions for someone arriving cold

Assume the repo owner has not seen the PR, review, code, or your run. Use everyday words: no internal symbols, types, file names, API names, review jargon, or links in the question or option meanings. Keep technical evidence reachable in a separate `Details:` section, not mixed into the decision.

Before asking, check the task description for recorded approval of the requested action or an `## Recorded answer` section. If it already grants the authorization or makes the decision needed, proceed and cite it in the PR body. Do not ask for decisions you are allowed to make, or use questions to hand back work that is merely hard. The answer authorizes exactly the question; it does not disable or bypass any check beyond what that question asked. One question per run: ask the most blocking decision and disclose any further decisions in the explanation. A declaration that carries a question is its own blocker record: do NOT also file `"$BOSS_BIN" propose blocked`, because that raises a separate attention item the answer never clears.

### Yes/No decision

Use `--question` for one specific Yes/No decision in plain language ("Can I proceed?" is not specific enough; put the justification in the explanation file, not the question). Write the labelled explanation to a file and pass `--explanation-file <path>` on a single line (`--explanation-file -` or `--explanation -` reads stdin; you may also pass `--explanation` with one quoted argument per line, and some PreToolUse guards reject commands containing newlines even inside quotes):

```
"$BOSS_BIN" propose done --outcome blocked --summary "<blocker>" --question "Approve switching the retry to exponential backoff?" --answer-type yes-no --explanation-file /tmp/explanation.txt
```

In the explanation, require these labels on their own lines, in this order (actual newlines, no Markdown heading decoration):

Context:
One or two plain-language sentences: what work you are doing and what problem forces this decision.
Yes:
What Yes does: the path the engine or the next worker will take, the user-visible result, and the rough size of the work.
No:
What No does: a different path the engine or the next worker will take, the user-visible result, and the rough size of that work. "No = remain blocked" is not a real choice. If you cannot name a second path, do not ask; proceed, or report the blocker without a question.
Recommendation:
I recommend Yes (or No) because <one-line reason>.
Details:
Optional technical evidence, symbols, file names, links, and review references. Omit this label if no detail is needed. Never truncate evidence just to make the question shorter.

The card presents Context, Question, Yes, No, Recommendation, then expandable Details. The run waits durably for the answer: Yes records the approval and redispatches; No records the declined path (granting no authorization) and redispatches so the next worker takes it.

### Choosing among several options

When the decision is a choice among several options rather than approve/decline, use `--answer-type multiple-choice` with two to six short, distinct, single-line labels via `--choices` (the card shows one button per label). Use only `Context:` (say what each option leads to), `Recommendation:` and optional `Details:` — no `Yes:` or `No:` sections (rejected for this type):

```
"$BOSS_BIN" propose done --outcome blocked --summary "<blocker>" --question "Which rollout should the migration use?" --answer-type multiple-choice --choices "Gradual" "All at once" --explanation-file /tmp/explanation.txt
```

The chosen label is recorded verbatim and the task restarts in the preserved workspace with an `## Recorded answer` section naming the question and the chosen option. Act on that choice; it grants no authorization beyond the decision itself.

### Waiting on another PR (prerequisite tasks)

When your task cannot finish until another PR lands first, propose a prerequisite task instead of a plain blocker, plain Yes/No, or `"$BOSS_BIN" propose followup-task` (which remains only the non-blocking path for nice-to-have work later). Use the same `Context` / `Yes` / `No` / `Recommendation` / `Details` format with a plain-language prerequisite name: explain the work and dependency in `Context`, and explain that Yes creates or links the prerequisite and waits while No records the declined path (granting no authorization) and redispatches. Do not pass `--question` — the engine words it for the repo owner.

- When no existing open task owns the work, create one (the prerequisite description can retain technical instructions and must stand alone for a different worker picking it up cold — name the files, failure and evidence, and what done looks like; never refer to "this task", "above", or your own session; if it names a PR already owned by another open task, the engine stores a link question instead):

```
"$BOSS_BIN" propose done --outcome blocked --summary "<blocker>" --answer-type create-prerequisite-task --prerequisite-name "<short task name>" --prerequisite-brief "<self-contained description>" --explanation-file /tmp/explanation.txt
```

- When an existing open task already owns the work (for example one owning an open PR), link it with `--prerequisite-pr` (URL, `owner/repo#n`, or PR number) and/or `--prerequisite-id` (canonical or friendly task id; never matched by name):

```
"$BOSS_BIN" propose done --outcome blocked --summary "<blocker>" --answer-type link-prerequisite-task --prerequisite-pr "<github-pr>" --explanation-file /tmp/explanation.txt
```

### Exceeding the `change/file-count` limit (Split vs Bypass limit)

Estimate the file count early from the target repo's documented per-change costs (not the number of deliverables); check the footprint once the shape is clear and again before final validation. When the change would exceed the `change/file-count` limit, stop adding scope and report a proposed split through the proposal below (do not file a plain blocker, plain Yes/No, or `followup-task`):

```
"$BOSS_BIN" propose done --outcome blocked --summary "<blocker>" --answer-type create-prerequisite-task --prerequisite-name "<first PR title>" --prerequisite-brief "<self-contained description of that first PR>" --file-count <n> --file-count-limit <limit> --explanation-file /tmp/explanation.txt
```

This is not a Yes/No question: the card shows buttons named **Split** and **Bypass limit**, and the engine words the question and owns those labels. Replace `Yes:` and `No:` with `Split:` and `Bypass limit:` in the order `Context`, `Split`, `Bypass limit`, `Recommendation`, `Details` (`--explanation` is refused if it still uses `Yes:` / `No:`). Never write "Yes" or "No" for these options. State the projected upper-bound file count (`--file-count`), the limit, and what lands in this PR versus the prerequisite or follow-up along capability boundaries. Recommend **Split** by default (`I recommend Split because <one-line reason>`); recommend **Bypass limit** only for the unsplittable cases named in the target repo's `CHECKS.yaml` (in spinyfin/mono, coordinated renames and generated trees).

**Split** creates the first coherent capability as a prerequisite PR and waits for it; **Bypass limit** authorizes a file-count exception for this PR only and redispatches so the next worker finishes in one PR by putting `BYPASS_CHANGE_FILE_COUNT=Split-vs-bypass question answered Bypass limit by the repo owner on <date>; one-PR exception for this PR only` (a free-form bypass reason without a numeric prefix, citing the recorded answer and date) in the commit description. Never apply that bypass without recorded authorization; it does not relax any other check. Checkleft does not enforce the approved count: before pushing, compare the non-deleted file count from `jj diff --stat` against the PR base with the approved bound and ask again if it is over.

Before submitting, read only Context, Question, the two options (Yes and No, or Split and Bypass limit), and Recommendation. Can someone who has never seen the work decide what each button will do? If not, rewrite those parts yourself. This writing contract does not change when a question is warranted or grant permission to bypass checks.

